{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,108p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '207,324p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '717,785p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '1162,1339p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '65,164p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,108p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '207,324p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '717,785p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '1162,1339p' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '65,164p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"# yin.vm.rust-kernel: the de Bruijn register kernel in Rust, and its LLVM lowering\n\nStatus: design only, revision 2 (2026-10-03). Not authorized for\nimplementation. Revision 2 reconciles two independent reviews\n(`deepseek-v4-pro`, `gpt-6.1-sol`): no native code touches a reference\ncount (4.1, 7.4); the shared image holds no `Value` (4.2, 4.6); a tail\nprimitive call always returns to the driver (7.3); no kernel fault can\ntake the node down (5.3, 5.5); register indices are 32-bit with a\ndeclared host limit (4.2); and the wire form of a return frame is stated\nto carry `:live` (4.4). This document designs a Rust port of the register\nexecution kernel `yin.vm.debruijn.register`\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc`, R4 of\n`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`), the role that Rust kernel plays as an\ninterpreter on `dao.stream`, and the strategy by which validated register\nimages are lowered to LLVM IR and run as native code beside it.\n\nIt is subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) (axioms and\ninvariants), [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\n(the register dimension, R, live sets, the sparse continuation),\n[`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md) section 7.3 (the offset table,\n`attach-image`, closure lift and lower),\n[`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) (content, refs,\nquery: what may and may not carry code), and\n[`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (the CESK reading of a\nlinear image and the observer/loader/image split).\n\nIt refines, and in four named places amends,\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md), which\nalready decided that a Rust host exists (T1), that it interprets the\nregister image (T-D13), and that it starts pure (Level 0) and grows to\noffload (Level 1, T5). That document was written before R1, R2 and R4\nlanded. They have landed: the Clojure kernel implements all 23 opcodes of\ncontract `\"r2\"` (register contract version 4), the engine seam, the\noffset table, and the module kernel. Section 11 lists what this changes\nin the targets design; where the two disagree and this document does not\nsay so explicitly, the targets document governs.\n\nLine numbers were read from the working tree on 2026-10-03.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Objective, scope, non-goals\n\nObjective. One Rust workspace that:\n\n1. executes validator-approved `:yin.debruijn.register/*` images with the\n   same observable behaviour as `register.cljc` under the B0 normalizer;\n2. is a peer interpreter on `dao.stream`: it observes a stream of images,\n   constructs CESK state, performs effects as stream emissions, and parks\n   and resumes continuations as plain data;\n3. can hand a validated image to LLVM and run the result, without the\n   native code becoming a new executable format, a new identity, or a\n   second continuation vocabulary.\n\nWhy the register kernel is the integration point for LLVM. Four\nproperties of the register image, all enforced by\n`register-image-defect` before execution\n(`debruijn_register_code.cljc:690-698`), are exactly the preconditions of\nan SSA lowering, and none of them holds for the stack image or the map\nAST:\n\n    +----------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n    | Register image property    | What LLVM lowering gets from it        |\n    +----------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n    | each body declares its     | a fixed-size activation; every         |\n    | register count; every      | register access is statically          |\n    | operand is bounds-checked  | in-bounds, no runtime check to emit    |\n    | (`register-bounds-rule`)   |                                        |\n    | jumps stay inside their    | one body is one function; its pcs are  |\n    | body (`jump-scope-rule`);  | its basic blocks; no cross-function    |\n    | bodies partition the pcs   | control flow exists to model           |\n    | operands are named         | instructions are already three-address |\n    | registers, no `:push`      | code; no stack-effect reconstruction   |\n    | every continuation         | the live set IS the spill map at that  |\n    | boundary carries an exact, | boundary: which registers must be      |\n    | receiver-recomputed `live` | written back to data, and that the     |\n    | set (`live-exact-rule`)    | rest are dead                          |\n    +----------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n\nThe last row is the decisive one. The project requires the continuation\nto be data at every observable point. A native compiler's hardest\nproblem under that requirement is knowing what to materialize where. The\nregister format answers it in-band, inside R's preimage, verified by\nevery receiver.\n\nScope. The kernel (`register.cljc` in full), the pure contract\nnamespaces it depends on (`debruijn_register_code`,\n`debruijn_register_effects`, the B1 scalar encoder), the engine seam as\nfar as each level needs it, and the LLVM lowering.\n\nNon-goals.\n\n- No new bytecode, no new identity, no change to R, H, the register\n  descriptor, the validator, or any `.cljc` namespace.\n- No Rust resolver or lowerer. The Rust node receives register images;\n  `resolve` and `lower-register` stay Clojure-side stages (targets\n  T-D2).\n- No speculative optimization, inline caching, or type feedback in this\n  revision (section 7.6).\n- No interop with any other language's runtime through LLVM. LLVM is a\n  code generator here, not a linking model for foreign libraries. The\n  only host boundary is `dao.stream` (`yin.vm.ffi.md`).\n- No stack-image kernel in Rust (targets T-D13).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Invariant review\n\n### 2.5 Dynamic extension\n\nCode arrives while the machine runs. The mechanism is the existing one:\n`attach-image` appends an admitted image to the code space and one row\nto the offset table; nothing already held moves (`register.cljc:193-227`).\nThe Rust kernel implements it with the same law. Native code follows the\nsame grain: an artifact is compiled per image row, keyed by the row's\nidentity, so attaching an image never invalidates native code for images\nalready held (7.5). Free-name resolution stays dynamic (`:load-free`\nresolves at run time in the order env, store, primitives, modules), which\nbounds what the translator may assume (7.6).\n\n### 2.6 Host isolation\n\nThe Rust process is a host like the JVM or Dart. Three isolation rules:\n\n1. Portable code never sees a Rust type. `Value` admits plain data, the\n   two kernel-minted types (closure, continuation), and resolved\n   primitives. No pointer, handle, or artifact address is a `Value`.\n2. The kernel crate contains no `unsafe`, performs no I/O, and does not\n   link LLVM. Everything that touches raw memory or the dynamic loader\n   lives in separate crates the kernel does not depend on (section 9).\n   A node with no LLVM toolchain is a complete node; it interprets.\n3. Host errors are classified into plain-data diagnostics at the point\n   they occur. A Rust panic is a kernel bug, not an outcome, and the\n   conformance suite fails on any panic. But a kernel bug must not be a\n   node outage: the kernel is written so that a violated internal\n   invariant is a `Refuse`, and a residual panic is contained to the one\n   machine it occurred in (5.5). The node never aborts on a machine's\n   fault.\n\n### 2.7 Design contradictions found\n\nFour contradictions between the brief, the existing designs, and the\ncode. Each is resolved here and listed again in section 11 for owner\nratification.\n\nC1. *Emitters never consume the register image* (targets T-D2,\n`yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md:1090-1097`) versus *the register kernel is\nthe integration point for LLVM* (the brief). Resolution: the LLVM\nlowering in this document is not a Direction A emitter. It is an\nexecution strategy inside a register kernel: a specialization of `step1`\nover one validated image. The targets document already draws this exact\nline for the JVM (\"closure-compiling the stack IMAGE at load time is a\ndifferent thing, an interpreter technique inside the existing host, and\nis not a lowering\", `targets.md:861-863`). The objection that motivated\nT-D2, that decoding a stack image reconstructs the expression tree, does\nnot apply: the register image needs no reconstruction (section 1 table).\nA Rust node holds no resolved tuples (T-D2 itself), so the image is the\nonly thing it could translate. T4, the source emitter from resolved\ntuples, is untouched and remains gated.\n\nC2. *Native artifacts never cross `dao.stream` as code* (targets T-D5,\n`targets.md:515-538`) versus *LLVM as a downstream worker on\n`dao.stream` that generates native code* (the brief). Resolution: the\nworker is a stream interpreter, and the streams it reads and writes are\nnode-local composition plumbing inside one trust domain. What crosses\nbetween nodes is unchanged: images by R, continuations as data. A\nreceiver still never executes an artifact it did not derive (8.3, 8.4).\n\nC3. *Everything is a continuation; the continuation is data* versus\n*LLVM function calls*. If a yin `:call` were lowered to an LLVM `call`,\nthe continuation would live in the native stack, unserializable.\nResolution: LLVM `call` is used only for runtime helpers that return\nbefore the next boundary. A yin call is a data transition followed by a\nreturn to the driver (7.3).\n\nC4. *Persistent `vm -> vm'`* (Clojure) versus *`&mut Machine`* (Rust). A\nClojure step that throws leaves the caller holding the old, intact VM.\nA Rust step that fails halfway through an in-place update would not.\nResolution: the check-then-commit rule of 5.3, tested by the conformance\nkit.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Topology\n\nThe compilation pipeline is a set of stream stages; each stage is an\ninterpreter that knows only its input and output values.\n\n    syntax --yang--> map AST --project--> rows (dao.jing)\n                                             |\n                             resolve (Clojure)\n                                             |\n                                     resolved tuples\n                                      /            \\\n                           lower-stack              lower-register\n                                |                        |\n                          stack image (H)         register image (R)\n                                |                        |\n                     stack kernels (B3)      register kernels:\n                                               R4 (CLJ, CLJS, CLJD)\n                                               Rust kernel (this doc)\n                                                    |          \\\n                                              interpret     translate (R -> IR text)\n                                                    ^             |\n                                                    |        LLVM worker (IR -> object)\n                                                    |             |\n                                                    +--- native artifact (local cache)\n\nThree statements about this picture:\n\n1. The Rust kernel is a peer of R4 below the format, not a new lowerer.\n   It consumes what R4 consumes.\n2. The translator and the LLVM worker are downstream of the image on the\n   same node, and upstream of nothing: no stage consumes native code as\n   input. Discarding every artifact loses nothing (it is a projection in\n   the sense of `code-as-tuples.md` section 1: derived, never an\n   identity, recomputed at will).\n3. The Rust kernel and any other observer of the image stream (an\n   indexer, a verifier, a Clojure kernel) are mutually ignorant. The\n   kernel never consults an index to run.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory layout\n\n### 4.1 Value\n## 6. Effects and the engine seam\n\nThe kernel/engine split is kept exactly. The kernel builds payloads with\nthe R2 constructors and hands effects to the engine; the engine decides\nvalue-or-park and owns the wait set. The three levels of targets section\n3.3 are kept, with the registers now sparse.\n\n### 6.1 Level 0: pure\n\nInstruction set: `const load-bound load-free closure move call\nbranch-false jump return halt store-get store-put define`. An image\ncontaining any other opcode is refused by a static scan before\nexecution. No stream, no park. This is targets T1 with `:define` added\n(contract `\"r2\"`).\n\n### 6.2 Level 1: offload\n\nThe Rust kernel is a remote step function for the Clojure engine. It\nruns until halt or until an effect or boundary instruction, then returns\nthe sparse payload plus the effect descriptor as data. Both are the pure\nR2 shapes: `effect-descriptor` and `continuation-payload`\n(`debruijn_register_effects.cljc:28-65`, `:83-119`). The Clojure side\nsupplies a restore of the engine's signature that forwards the payload\nand the woken value back. The Rust side holds no wait set, no handle, no\nscheduler.\n\nBecause R4 now exists, a payload parked by the Rust kernel must restore\nunder R4 and the reverse, byte for byte in the payload's canonical\nencoding. Targets 3.3 called that fallback \"not promised\"; with a\nfirst-party kernel it is the completion criterion (section 10, K2).\n\n### 6.3 Level 2: standalone\n\nThe Rust node owns the engine. Ported as a separate crate, in this\norder, each a parity target against `engine.cljc`:\n\n1. `handle-effect` and the stream handlers over a `dao.stream` handle\n   trait whose operations return the exhaustive outcome map.\n2. `check-wait-set`, `scheduler-round`, `resume-from-run-queue`, the\n   terminal-outcome check before restore, `run-loop`\n   (`engine.cljc:1658-1672`).\n3. `park-continuation`, `resume-continuation`, `gensym`, ref sealing\n   (`issue-ref`, `authentic-ref?`).\n4. The FFI two-step over the `dao.stream.apply` pair. The request and\n   response envelopes are `dao.stream.apply` data; nothing in this crate\n   names a transport.\n5. The heap and its collector, with the same allocation-count trigger\n   and the same never-reused ids, because collection timing is\n   observable (2.4).\n6. The module kernel (`IModuleKernel`, `register.cljc:806-875`):\n   `lift-closure`, `lower-closure`, `spawn-module`, `gc-roots`,\n   `gc-children`, and the link-install path.\n\nRestore is `register-restore` (`register.cljc:402-430`) with its four\ngates in order: format and row identity (`:image`, never `:hash`),\n`continuation-defect`, plain-data resume value, then the FFI two-step or\n`write-back`.\n\nThe wait set, ready queue and parked map hold plain data only. A Rust\nnode's blocked machine must survive a canonical-encoding round trip and\nrestore on a JVM node running R4. No `Waker`, future, channel, or\nclosure is stored in an entry. The kernel and engine crates are\nsynchronous; they do not depend on an async runtime. Cadence (when to\ncall `run` again) belongs to the host driver, and liveness to\n`dao.lease`, as on every other host.\n\n---\n\n## 7. LLVM bridging strategy\n## 10. Phases\n\nEach phase adds files only under `src/host/rust/` and `test/`; none\nedits an existing `.cljc` namespace.\n\n    +----+-------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    | Ph | Deliverable                   | Depends on                     |\n    +----+-------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    | K0 | host conformance kit (targets | R1, R2, R4: all landed         |\n    |    | T0), exported from R4         |                                |\n    | K1 | Rust kernel, Level 0 (targets | K0                             |\n    |    | T1), incl. attach-image       |                                |\n    | K2 | effects tier as offload,      | K1                             |\n    |    | Level 1 (targets T5)          |                                |\n    | K3 | benchmark report: K1 vs R4 on | K1                             |\n    |    | JVM vs whole-node AOT of two  |                                |\n    |    | hand-translated corpus images |                                |\n    | K4 | translator, inline runtime,   | K2; K3 reviewed by the owner   |\n    |    | worker, native path           |                                |\n    | K5 | standalone node, Level 2      | K2; dao.stream Rust client     |\n    |    | (targets T6)                  | (not designed here)            |\n    +----+-------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n\nK0. The kit targets T0 specified does not exist\n(`test/resources/yin/debruijn/conformance/` is absent). It is the first\ndeliverable, and it is simpler than T0 planned: the oracle is R4 itself,\nso the test-only reference interpreter (targets T-D14) is unnecessary.\nThe kit exports, per corpus program: the image as canonical data, R, the\nB0-normalized result or error, and the format-level trace R4 produces\n(payload at every boundary, K depth, saved pairs). Plus: refusal\nfixtures for all fourteen validator rules with rule keywords, the\ndescriptor hash, scalar-encoding vectors, diagnostic message fixtures,\natomicity fixtures (a refused step leaves the state equal), tail-site\nfixtures for every row of the 7.3 table (a tail primitive value and a\ntail primitive effect returning into a caller in another body, and\nhalting with K empty), a wide-body fixture whose register count exceeds\n65,535, host-limit refusal fixtures, and the million-iteration tail\nfixtures.\n\nK1 completion: every pure fixture passes; R equal; refusals equal by\nrule; no recursion in `step1` or `run`; constant native stack and one\nfile per live activation on the tail fixtures; atomicity fixtures pass;\nno panic; a fault-injection test (a deliberately corrupted decoded\nimage, bypassing the validator) yields `:kernel-invariant` refusals and\na poisoned machine at worst, never a process exit, while a second\nmachine on the same node runs to completion.\n\nK2 completion: for every effect fixture, the Rust kernel's payload and\neffect descriptor equal R4's in canonical encoding, every return frame's\n`:live` included; a payload parked by\nRust restores under R4 and one parked by R4 restores under Rust, to the\nsame normalized outcome; `continuation-defect` refusals equal by rule.\n\nK3 is a gate in the sense targets gave T4: numbers the owner reads\nbefore K4 is commissioned. Section 7.6 predicts a bounded gain; K3 is\nwhere that prediction is checked before any LLVM code is written.\n\nK4 completion: translator output byte-identical across runs and\nplatforms; the equivalence law of 7.2 over the whole kit, native versus\ninterpreted, including payload equality at every boundary and diagnostic\nequality on refusal; artifact dropped mid-run at a boundary continues\ninterpreted to the same result; a node with no toolchain passes the\nwhole kit interpreted and records `:unsupported`.\n\nK5 completion: the engine parity criteria of 6.3; a blocked Rust machine\nround-trips through its wire form and resumes on a JVM node, and the\nreverse.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Decisions, amendments, and owner questions\n\nDECIDED in this document (within the brief's scope):\n\n1. K-D1. The Rust kernel is a port of `register.cljc` at contract\n   `\"r2\"`, a peer of R4, consuming validated register images only.\n2. K-D2. `Value` is a 16-byte `repr(C, u8)` enum in safe Rust; closures\n   and continuations are host types with owner tags; stream, cursor and\n   cell refs are plain data. Only the tag and immediate payloads are\n   layout visible outside safe Rust; no native code touches a reference\n   count.\n   K-D2a. The shared `Image` holds plain data only and is `Send + Sync`\n   by derivation; each machine owns its constant pool.\n   K-D2b. Indices are `u32`; anything larger, or beyond the host's\n   declared limits, is refused at load with `:host-limit`.\n3. K-D3. Return frames and payloads are sparse (live registers only),\n   carry no code space, and restore by row identity.\n4. K-D4. `step1` is check-then-commit; a refused step leaves the machine\n   unchanged. A violated internal invariant is a `:kernel-invariant`\n   refusal, a residual panic poisons one machine, and the node never\n   aborts (5.5).\n5. K-D5. LLVM lowering is a specialization of the kernel over one\n   validated image row, with register pcs as its only resume labels and\n   the interpreter's transition functions as its runtime. A yin call is\n   never an LLVM call.\n6. K-D6. Inline tier by LTO over the tag and immediate payloads only;\n   out-of-line tier, including every clone and drop of a counted value,\n   by an explicit function table argument. No artifact contains kernel code or\n   resolves a symbol against the process.\n7. K-D7. Artifacts are node-local caches keyed by `(row identity,\n   target, translator version, ABI version)`; the worker is reached only\n   by streams; the kernel never waits on it.\n8. K-D8. The kernel, image, value and engine crates forbid `unsafe`,\n   link no LLVM, and use no async runtime.\n\nAMENDMENTS proposed to `yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md`:\n\n- A1. Its status header (`targets.md:3-15`) and section 3.7.1 describe\n  R1 and R4 as unbuilt and unauthorized. Both are built. T0's\n  dependency is met and T-D14's reference interpreter is superseded by\n  R4 as the oracle.\n- A2. Section 3.2's Rust types are superseded by section 4 here (sparse\n  frames, owner tags, `store_of`, offset table, plain-data refs).\n- A3. T-D2 gains a clause: a kernel-side translation of an admitted\n  register image is an execution strategy of that kernel, not an\n  emitter, and is permitted (contradiction C1).\n- A4. T1's \"no `unsafe`\" is scoped to the non-native crates (section 9).\n\nOWNER QUESTIONS:\n\n1. Ratify C1/A3: may a register kernel translate the register image it\n   holds, or must all native code come from resolved tuples (T4), which\n   a standalone Rust node does not have?\n2. Ratify C2: the LLVM worker's streams are node-local and artifacts\n   never cross nodes. Is a shared compile farm wanted later, with a\n   `:trusted` policy?\n3. Should the worker's request/outcome vocabulary be `dao.stream.apply`\n   envelopes (8.3)?\n4. The K3 threshold for commissioning K4.\n5. K1 scalar classes: the eleven-class common domain first (targets\n   T-D6), with bigint, ratio and the float64 kind following?\n\nDEFERRED: a kernel-owned `repr(C)` counted object header that would\npermit inline retain and release (it moves the counting implementation\ninto an unsafe crate, so it needs a K3 number showing the out-of-line\ncalls matter); guaranteed tail calls between native bodies; SSA registers\nwith live-set write-back; guarded specialization and deoptimization;\nin-process JIT; cross-node artifact sharing; the Rust `dao.stream`\nclient and transports; WASM as an artifact target — superseded by [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) (register-image-to-wasm lowering), [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5, guest-agnostic execution host, `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI, and K0/W0 conformance method), and [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10, register wasm execution instance and K0/W0 register fixtures) (owner-authorized wasm plan, 2026-10-18 / 2026-10-19).\n\n---\n\n## 12. Risks\n\n1. Primitive and equality drift. Unchanged from targets section 9 and\n   still the largest surface. The kit is the only defence.\n2. Two dispatchers. The interpreter and native code must agree forever.\n   Mitigation is structural (one set of transition functions, 7.4) and\n   tested (every fixture both ways, 7.2). A transition implemented twice\n   is a defect.\n3. Layout coupling. The inline tier fixes `Value`'s tag position and\n   immediate payload offset as ABI, and nothing else: no reference\n   count offset and no `Rc` representation. A change is an ABI version\n   bump that\n   invalidates every artifact; because artifacts are caches, that costs\n   recompilation only.\n4. Toolchain coupling. LTO needs the inline bitcode and the worker's\n   LLVM to match. Pinned by the ABI version; mismatch degrades to\n   interpretation.\n5. Modest speedup. Dynamic resolution and boxed arithmetic bound what\n   revision 1 can gain (7.6). K3 exists so this is measured before K4\n   is built.\n6. Engine port size. `engine.cljc` is over two thousand lines including\n   linking, sealing and collection. Level 2 is the expensive phase and\n   is last for that reason; Level 1 gives effectful programs without it.\n7. Unsafe surface. Loading native code is the one place a defect can\n   corrupt a machine rather than refuse. It is confined to three crates,\n   optional, and exercised only on images this node validated.\n8. Payload size. The payload carries its code space by value\n   (`:segment`). That is the existing contract, not introduced here; a\n   Rust node shares the `Arc<Image>` in memory and pays the cost only on\n   the wire.\n\nRelated specs: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5, in-instance engine, wasm implementation of dao.stream, and K0/W0 conformance method); [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) (register-image-to-wasm lowering); [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10, register wasm execution instance, K0/W0 register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates).\n\n2026-10-18: Added the owner-authorized wasm-plan and shared-kit pointers; revision 2 status and all other normative content are unchanged.\n2026-10-19: Owner-directed decomposition and fold of the 2026-10-18 wasm consensus into yang.wasm.md (lowering), yin.vm.engine.md §E.1–§E.5 (guest-agnostic execution host, service protocol yin.vm.engine.host.v1, and conformance method), and yin.vm.debruijn.register.md §10 (register wasm execution instance, register fixture schema, and register W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates), retiring yin.vm.register-wasm.md and yin.vm.conformance-kit.md; revision 2 status and line coordinates 1–1334 are unchanged.\n2026-10-19 owner boundary clarification: the wasm plan now depends on compiling the K-phase Rust crates to wasm32-unknown-unknown as its in-instance engine; the composition supplies only dao.stream adapters. This pointer supersedes the historical service-protocol wording in the line-1300 pointer and earlier footer; revision 2 design-only status, file name and coordinates 1–1334 remain unchanged. Owner taxonomy clarification, 2026-10-19: dao.stream is the abstraction boundary; ring-buffer, websocket, UDP and wasm import surfaces are implementations, including co-resident in-instance streams without host crossings.\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**The system ABI.** One abstraction boundary (`dao.stream`), many\nimplementations (ring-buffer, websocket, UDP, wasm imports). `dao.stream` is\nthe contract for datom.world and `yin.vm`: operations, exhaustive outcome maps,\ncanonical values, append-only batch semantics and observer discipline. It is\nnot a transport implementation or a wire protocol. The wasm import-surface\nimplementation is specified once in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3;\nits canonical Jing CBOR representation does not define the abstraction itself.\n\n**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):** “dao.stream is an abstraction\nboundary. It is not a transport implementation. It can be implemented using a\nring-buffer, a websocket or UDP”\n\nThe wasm import surface is another implementation: adapters implement the\nabstraction through imported functions over linear memory. A co-resident\nproducer can instead use an in-memory ring-buffer implementation inside the\ninstance with zero host crossings. Both preserve the same abstraction contract.\n\nWhat crosses a trust domain is a strict subset of in-machine values: closures\nand continuations cross only as admissible portable plain data; host types,\nresources and code artifacts never do. The custody gates retain this distinction\n([yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md)); an implementation grants no additional\nportability or authority.\n\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found;\none abstraction boundary is the principle.** The boundary between wasm and datom.world is\n`dao.stream`; its implementation uses whatever wasm exposes to the outside\nworld. The exposed functions are **adapters to dao.stream**, which is the ABI\nto `yin.vm` and the whole ecosystem. Machine-internal work executes inside the\nwasm instance: the Rust engine crates and compiled register bodies share memory\nand direct calls. Only effects/facts cross through the stream adapters. World\nfacts still require interaction with the world; moving engine implementation\ninside the machine does not manufacture those facts.\n\n**Dated supersession (owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19):** This replaces\nthe earlier same-day “sole justification … performance” allowance for\nprocess-local services and the service-protocol interpretation of\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`). Its private operation vocabulary, TLV codec,\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator and service-status protocol are dissolved.\nPerformance work optimizes internal execution or the stream implementation; it does\nnot reintroduce a semantic service interface. [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md)\nis the compiler implementation plan for this in-instance architecture.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '160,180p' docs/design/datom.world.md; rg -n 'rust-kernel|lines |:[0-9]|§E\\\\.3' docs/design/{yin.vm.engine,yin.vm.debruijn.register,yang.wasm}.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,3} '\"' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '435,465p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '440,620p' docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '160,180p' docs/design/datom.world.md; rg -n 'rust-kernel|lines |:[0-9]|§E\\\\.3' docs/design/{yin.vm.engine,yin.vm.debruijn.register,yang.wasm}.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,3} '\"' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md; sed -n '435,465p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '440,620p' docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:262:    |   wait-set then resume     | inlines it, the semantic VM names it      |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:363:now operate inside wasm; §E.3 replaces the dissolved protocol; §E.4 hosts only\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:370:[yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md), [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:433:explicitly not NaN-boxed (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:324–392, 1238–1244`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:439:materializing constants per task (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:394–432, 553–563`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:471:(`debruijn_register_code.cljc:258–271`). Do not replace it with a CBOR hash\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:475:(`debruijn_code.cljc:198–203, 224–242, 350–378, 410–465`). The native backend\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:507:`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1136–1145`, extended for the current\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:644:Node/browser, Wasmtime and Wasmer provide the same stream implementation (§E.3).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:664:(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`), compiled to\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:671:compiled wasm bodies and future LLVM helpers (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:676:mandatory (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`). Panics are kernel defects,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:680:(lines 1154–1158).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:683:DEFERRED wasm artifact item (lines 1294–1300), not the K3 review gate for K4 or\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:687:R is executable checksum despite older Rust topology wording (lines 282–318,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:788:artifact-cache policy (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011, 1113–1122,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:808:[`yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md), this specification\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:812:second kit (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`, equivalence law at\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:813:`:805–831`). The execution specifications retain their respective\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:953:(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1167–1182, 1215–1228`). Full register slice\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:978:original lines 1–1334. Adapter names in §E.3.1 are proposed imports for this implementation.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:18:implementation specified in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Its former\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:23:implementation specified in engine §E.3; a co-resident in-instance ring-buffer\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:42:- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:121:[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2 is an authority for\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:122:the native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:132:(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`). Its other deferred items and K3-owner-review\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:136:- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines 282–318; §7.5, lines 997–1011). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:137:- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:138:- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines 93–95, 1164–1165). The deterministic engine traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:139:- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines 677–678). Keep that rule for the Rust interpreter lane. The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a different dispatcher used to verify its generated execution; it does not silently replace Rust's public `step`. Optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:140:- The in-instance wasm engine needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines 748–781; §10, lines 1170–1182). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); a Level-1 offload service cannot satisfy in-instance wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:142:Owner question 1 (lines 1282–1284) and C1/A3 (lines 243–258, 1275–1277) align with the owner's\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:220:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Components share one task-owned,\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:277:`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:805–831` (§7.2): from equal reachable boundary states they\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:280:instructions differ, their guest semantics do not (§7.3, lines 833–918). W0’s shared kit tests\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:397:home: [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. The compiler emits no private\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:480:Artifact policy is also shared with LLVM: `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:997–1011,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:562:values in long-running stream pipelines and actor loops as unaccounted\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:767:of which the stack design's section 1 already declines to promise.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:889:pipelines and actor loops can park indefinitely; retaining dead values at\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1249:- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, `Value` ownership, transition reuse, and LLVM sibling relationship.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1259:only dao.stream adapters (§E.3) cross into the composition. The single-port rule\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1372:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1428:(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`). The guest-agnostic differential\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1446:- tail-site fixtures for every row of the Rust design's §7.3 call-outcome table (including a tail primitive value and a tail primitive effect returning into a caller in another body, and halting with `K` empty; `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:833–918`),\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1681:(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1201–1228`):\n1:# yin.vm.rust-kernel: the de Bruijn register kernel in Rust, and its LLVM lowering\n42:## 1. Objective, scope, non-goals\n107:## 2. Invariant review\n113:### 2.1 Foundational invariants\n155:### 2.2 Ownership boundaries\n179:### 2.3 Explicit state and control flow\n187:### 2.4 Concurrency and linearization\n207:### 2.5 Dynamic extension\n219:### 2.6 Host isolation\n238:### 2.7 Design contradictions found\n282:## 3. Topology\n322:## 4. Memory layout\n324:### 4.1 Value\n394:### 4.2 Code space\n459:### 4.3 Activation: the register file and frames\n481:### 4.4 Continuation\n520:### 4.5 The machine\n553:### 4.6 Threads\n568:## 5. The CESK execution loop\n570:### 5.1 Configuration\n578:### 5.2 Transitions\n639:### 5.3 The step function and its atomicity rule\n680:### 5.4 Diagnostics\n688:### 5.5 Fault containment\n717:## 6. Effects and the engine seam\n724:### 6.1 Level 0: pure\n732:### 6.2 Level 1: offload\n748:### 6.3 Level 2: standalone\n785:## 7. LLVM bridging strategy\n787:### 7.1 Components\n805:### 7.2 The equivalence law\n833:### 7.3 Function shape and calls\n920:### 7.4 Function calls and LTO\n997:### 7.5 Artifact identity and the code cache\n1013:### 7.6 What the translator may not assume\n1038:## 8. The Rust node as an interpreter on dao.stream\n1040:### 8.1 Program ingress\n1068:### 8.2 Peer, not server\n1083:### 8.3 The LLVM worker\n1113:### 8.4 Trust\n1124:### 8.5 Effects out, events in\n1134:## 9. Crate layout\n1162:## 10. Phases\n1232:## 11. Decisions, amendments, and owner questions\n1304:## 12. Risks\nfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\nit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\ncomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\npolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\nthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\nparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\ncontract-generic may be built on it.\n\nA handle may be composed over another handle on the same logical stream: it\ndeclares that stream's identity, implements no surface the inner handle\nlacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\nown interpretation. Such a handle is host-composed, like a transport\nconstructor's closures, and is otherwise an ordinary handle.\n\nThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\none positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\nre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\npromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\nhost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\ntransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\ntransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\nits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\ndefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\npublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\ntakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\nconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\nspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n\nA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\none permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an\n  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the\n  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed\n  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting\n  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,\n  like any storage failure.)\n- The destination must also be able to **carry every envelope this transport\n  deposits** — attachment identifiers, lifecycle events, and every value the\n  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers\n  `:dao.stream/invalid-value` on some envelope is as unwired as a reject-mode\n  one; retention mode alone is not admission.\n- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused\n  when the boundary is composed. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before\n  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.\n- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit\n  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is\n  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination\n  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;\n  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary\n  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.\n  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the\n  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and\n  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary\n  `attach!`.\n\nSizing the window is choosing how much cursor lag to tolerate before gaps\nappear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Whether\nthat window can be resized after the fact is the destination transport's\ndeclared nature, not this transport's business. An adaptive policy — grow on\nrising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own\ntelemetry, not a transport behavior.\n\nThe accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention\npressure surfaces as `gap`s to cursors that span the eviction, and a dead\ndestination tears the\nattachment down. But a deposit that fails is a deposit that did not land, and\nneither can the teardown event land in the destination that just refused it —\nso in that one case the local failure is observable only through the deposit\nresult the adapter holds and through subsequent handle operations, and the\npeer observes it only if teardown reaches the wire.\n\n**Outbound bounds.** The handle's own `append!` is bounded in the other\ndirection, by `make-attacher` / `make-endpoint` config (positive integers or\nnil for unbounded), judged after encode and before the send:\n\n- `:ws/outbound-high-water`: at or above it, `append!` answers the transient\n  `:dao.stream/full` and sends nothing. A `dao.stream.remote.md` mirror\n  rewinds an idempotent answer on `full` and re-answers it next tick (2.3).\n- `:ws/max-outbound-bytes`: at or above it, the connection is torn down:\n  close 1008 `dao.stream/outbound-overflow`, `:ws/error` reason\n  `:ws/outbound-overflow`, phase closed, and `append!` answers\n  `:dao.stream/closed` then and afterwards. A peer that requests and never\n  reads therefore cannot grow this host's outbound memory without bound.\n\nThe level judged is the host's outbound backlog, read from the\n`{:send! :close!}` seam's optional `:queued-bytes` (a no-argument function).\nWhere a host has no such signal the seam omits it: `:ws/outbound-high-water`\ndoes not apply, and `:ws/max-outbound-bytes` is a cumulative quota of\nencoded bytes sent since open, with the same teardown. Per host:\n\n| host seam | `:queued-bytes` | outbound bounds | inbound frame bound | lift status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| JVM client (`java.net.http`) | the send chain's own count, raised at submission, lowered at completion | exact | reassembly bounded, `:too-large!`, then abort | exact |\n| JVM server (http-kit) | none | cumulative quota | http-kit `:max-ws`, plus the transport | gated |\n| Node (`ws`) client and server | `bufferedAmount` | exact | `maxPayload`, plus the transport | exact |\n| browser (DOM `WebSocket`) | `bufferedAmount` | exact | the transport, on whole messages | gated |\n| Dart (`dart:io` `WebSocket`) | none | cumulative quota | the transport, on whole messages | gated |\n\nA gated host stays gated for the loopback lift until its bounds and teardown\nare demonstrated.\n\n**Host wire codes.** The close codes above are what the transport asks its\nseam for; two hosts cannot put them on the wire, and their seams say so:\n\n- Browser: DOM `WebSocket.close` accepts only 1000 or 3000–4999 and throws\n  `InvalidAccessError` for anything else, closing nothing. The seam sends 1008,\n  1009, and 1013 as 1000 with the transport's reason string, so the socket\n  closes and its `close` event delivers the terminal. The peer sees 1000 plus\n  the reason, not the overflow code; the qualified `:ws/error` reason on the\n  local stream is unchanged. Demonstrated against a fake enforcing the DOM\n  contract, not yet in a real browser, so the browser stays gated.\n- JVM client: `java.net.http.WebSocket.sendClose` rejects 1009 and 1013\n  (among others). The seam does not chain such a close behind pending sends;\n  it aborts the socket at once and reports the terminal locally with the\n  requested code and reason. Sends the abort fails report nothing further.\n  The peer sees the connection drop without a close frame (its 1006), never\n  1009. Reassembly retains at most the bound plus one fragment before the\n  abort; the cleared buffers keep their capacity until the aborted\n  connection is released. 1013 is only ever sent by served connections, so\n  the client never hits it in practice.\n\n## Envelope\n\nTransport-owned keys are qualified under `:ws/…`; the logical-stream identity\nkey is contract-owned. The exact key set is settled by the descriptor gate;\nthese properties constrain it:\n\n- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it\n  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — advertised host,\n  advertised port, canonical path, perhaps TLS parameters — plus the distinct\n  `:dao.stream/identity`. Endpoint address alone does not name a stream.\n- An endpoint constructor receives bind host/port and advertised host/port as\n  separate composition data. Bind defaults to `127.0.0.1`. An omitted\n  advertised host defaults to a concrete bind host, but a wildcard bind\n  requires an explicit advertised host. An omitted advertised port defaults to\n  the actual bound port, including the assigned port after a bind to zero.\n  Descriptors returned by accepted handles use the advertised values.\n- The descriptor carries reachability and logical-stream identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the\n  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no\n  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.\n\n## The Handshake\n\nAttaching presents the descriptor's canonical lookup path, and the endpoint\neither accepts it or **authoritatively disclaims** it. Both halves are wire protocol\nowned by this transport, not by any composition: a client must recognize the\ndisclaimer to deposit `:ws/not-found` rather than a generic failure, so a\ncomposition inventing its own would not interoperate with any client. What a\ncomposition owns is only which identities it serves — its resolution table —\nand never the vocabulary of asking and disclaiming.\n\nAttaching presents `:ws/path` as the WebSocket HTTP request-target path.\n`:ws/path` is a canonical, non-empty absolute path beginning with `/`. URI dot\nsegments are removed; percent-encoded unreserved octets are decoded; remaining\npercent hex digits are upper-case; an empty URI path becomes `/`; and encoded\nslashes remain encoded. Repeated slashes and a trailing slash are significant.\nQuery and fragment components are ignored for lookup and are absent from the\ndescriptor. Resolution is an exact string lookup on this canonical form.\n\nSubprotocol selection is explicit on both ends, because the wire codec is a\ncomposition choice carried as a **codec profile**: the subprotocol name it\nnegotiates, its frame kind (`:text` or `:binary`), the portable-value\npredicate for its domain, and its encoder and decoder. Two profiles exist —\n`dao.stream.transit-json` (text frames) and `dao.stream.cbor` (binary\nframes) — and both run the same state machine, envelopes, and lifecycle. A\nclient offers exactly the subprotocol of its selected profile; an endpoint\nserves exactly the profiles it was composed with, concurrently and through\nthe same served paths. The endpoint negotiates deterministically: a named\noffer it does not serve is refused before the upgrade completes, never\nsilently downgraded to a codec the client did not select, and an absent\nname is the `dao.stream.transit-json` default for sockets that negotiated\nnothing.\n\nAfter upgrade, the first WebSocket message sent by the server is exactly one\nframe in the negotiated codec — one Transit-JSON text frame or one CBOR\nbinary frame:\n\n```clojure\n{:ws/frame :ws/accept}\n```\n\nor:\n\n```clojure\n{:ws/frame :ws/disclaim}\n```\n\n`:ws/accept` authoritatively accepts the presented path and causes the\nclient boundary to deposit `:ws/opened`. `:ws/disclaim` authoritatively\ndisclaims it and causes the client boundary to deposit `:ws/not-found`; the\nserver then closes with code `4004` and reason `dao.stream/not-found`. The\nclient does not deposit `:ws/opened` merely because the HTTP upgrade\ncompleted.\n\nPath resolution and `:ws/disclaim` happen in the upgrade callback. A\nnot-found connection never occupies an acceptance slot and does not wait for\n`endpoint-step`; slot-exhaustion teardown is likewise immediate and bounded by\nthe host's upgrade queue.\n\nNo value frame may be sent before `:ws/accept`. A frame of any other shape in\nthe first position is a protocol failure. If the connection closes before\neither `:ws/accept` or `:ws/disclaim` is received, the client deposits\n`:ws/transport-error` as that attachment's resolution.\n\n## Operations\n\n- There is no `create!`. The transport owns no stream, so it creates none;\n  a host serves a stream it composed elsewhere, and starting an endpoint is\n  a host composition step, not a DaoStream operation. A creation\n  specification naming this transport returns `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n- `attach!` begins connecting and returns without waiting, per the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The current C1/C2 sections address image lowering and local artifact caches; they don’t describe the dissolved wasm service protocol. I’ll retain those conclusions, remove their obsolete ratification framing, and supersede the Level-1 offload role. I’ll keep engine §E.3 as a pointer so the surviving §E.4/§E.5 references stay stable.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nD=Path('docs/design'); names=['datom.world','yin.vm.engine','yin.vm.debruijn.register','yang.wasm','yin.vm.rust-kernel','dao.stream']; old={n:(D/(n+'.md')).read_text() for n in names}; out=dict(old)\ndef part(s,a,b):return s[s.index(a):s.index(b)]\ndef block(s,a,b,t):return s[:s.index(a)]+t+'\\\\n\\\\n'+s[s.index(b):]\nmaster=old['datom.world']; doctrine=part(master,'A host boundary is a stream boundary','## Architectural Commitments')\ndoctrine=doctrine.replace('[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3','[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)')\n# preserve moved text, partition into repository-style sections\nfor a,b in [('**The system ABI.**','## 1. The system ABI\\\\n\\\\n'),('**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):**','## 2. Implementations and the taxonomy ruling\\\\n\\\\n**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):**'),('What crosses a trust domain','## 3. Custody at the boundary\\\\n\\\\nWhat crosses a trust domain'),('**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19:','## 4. Boundary ruling and supersession\\\\n\\\\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19:'),('**Effects out.**','## 6. Effects out and events in\\\\n\\\\n**Effects out.**'),('**Adapters.**','## 7. Adapter discipline\\\\n\\\\n**Adapters.**')]:doctrine=doctrine.replace(a,b)\npos=doctrine.index('## 6. Effects out')\ndoctrine=doctrine[:pos]+'''## 5. Naming and the floor\n\n“The system ABI” names only \"'`dao.stream`. Earlier qualified guest-host service\nnames are historical provenance for the dissolved protocol, not names for a\nsecond contract. Implementation specs name their implementation (WebSocket,\nwasm import surface), never “the wasm ABI”. Internal compiler linkage does not\ncreate an ecosystem boundary.\n\nThe floor is the need to obtain world facts that shipped code cannot compute.\nThat seam is immovable; the engineering frontier is movable: machine-internal\noperations can move inside the instance without changing the abstraction.\nAn in-instance stream between co-resident producers and observers needs no host\ncrossing. Changing physical placement does not remove external facts or custody.\n\nImplementations include ring-buffer, [WebSocket](./dao.stream.ws.md),\n[wasm imports](./dao.stream.wasm.md), and UDP. These implement one abstraction;\nnone adds an ABI. The import-surface representation and buffer rules belong\nonly to its implementation spec.\n\n'\"'''+doctrine[pos:]\nabi='''# datom.world ABI: the one abstraction boundary\n\nStatus: design/doctrine; owner rulings, 2026-10-19.\n\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md) owns **what** the contract is: operations,\nexhaustive outcome maps, canonical values, batch and observer semantics. This\ndocument owns **why** that contract is the only boundary: role, rulings,\nimplementation taxonomy, floor and custody. It does not redefine the operations.\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): distilled from this session's owner rulings and moved\nfrom datom.world.md, Host Boundaries. The five-round wasm consensus lineage\nis recorded in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md); subsequent owner rulings below\nsupersede its former service-protocol framing. This is the single doctrine home.\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): master axioms and commitments.\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the abstraction contract.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): execution, custody and conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): concrete machine state and fixtures.\n\n'''+doctrine\nout['datom.world']=block(master,'### Host Boundaries','## Architectural Commitments','''### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.''')\ne=old['yin.vm.engine']; adapter=part(e,'### E.3 The wasm implementation','### E.4 ')\n# single-home doctrine: implementation only points to ruling, retained technical paragraphs untouched\nadapter=adapter[adapter.index('#### E.3.1 '):]\nadapter=adapter.replace('#### E.3.1 Imported adapter surface','## 1. Imported adapter surface').replace('#### E.3.2 Outcome and buffer discipline','## 2. Outcome and buffer discipline').replace('#### E.3.3 Canonical bytes and authority','## 3. Canonical bytes and authority').replace('Jing’s canonical CBOR (§E.2.2)', 'Jing’s canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2)').replace(\\\"Jing's canonical CBOR (§E.2.2)\\\",\\\"Jing's canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2)\\\")\nwasm='''# DaoStream Wasm: the wasm implementation of the dao.stream abstraction\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented. Subordinate to\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): that document is the contract; this one\nspecifies the wasm import-surface implementation. Where they disagree, the\ncontract wins. It is a sibling implementation spec to\n[dao.stream.ws.md](./dao.stream.ws.md).\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): moved without changing the adapter contract from\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 by owner-approved decomposition.\nDoctrine and the dated supersessions have one home,\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md). The prior service-protocol tables\nremain dissolved; this move does not revive them.\n\nThe import surface exists because wasm reaches outside through imported\nfunctions over linear memory. These adapters implement dao.stream operations.\nThe specification is language-neutral: CLJS hosts and native embedders implement\nthe same surface. The Rust host-side implementation plan lives in\n[yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md) §6.4; the guest engine is not\nimplemented by those host adapters.\n\nWith co-resident producers, an in-instance ring-buffer implementation may\nservice stream operations with zero host crossings. The signatures and Jing\nbytes below specify the import-surface implementation, not a requirement that\nevery implementation serialize or cross into the host. Canonical encoding is\nowned by [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md); engine codec conformance is specified in\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2 and §E.5.\n\n'''+adapter\nout['yin.vm.engine']=block(e,'### E.3 The wasm implementation','### E.4 ','''### E.3 Wasm stream implementation reference\n\nThe adapter surface, import signatures, outcome encoding, canonical Jing bytes\nand buffer discipline live in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §§1–3.\nOwner decomposition, 2026-10-19: the former §E.3 content moved there unchanged;\n§E.4 and §E.5 retain their numbers. Boundary doctrine and supersessions live in\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md).''')\n# doctrine duplicates in existing notes become pointers; architectural supersessions stay\ns=out['yin.vm.engine'];s=s.replace('Historical rename \"'`yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is provenance,'\"\\\\nnot a live interface. The performance exception, private third codec/comparator,\\\\ntransport statuses and deferred W6 engine migration are superseded.', 'The historical rename and boundary/taxonomy supersessions are recorded only in\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §4. Deferred W6 engine migration\\\\nremains superseded by the in-instance architecture.')\nout['yin.vm.engine']=s\n# Rust rescope, core sections preserved\nr=old['yin.vm.rust-kernel']; orig_r=r\nr=block(r,'# yin.vm.rust-kernel:','## 1. Objective, scope, non-goals','''# yin.vm.rust-engine: Rust engine implementation plan\n\nStatus: design only; not implemented. Revision 2 technical core (2026-10-03),\nrescoped by owner ruling on 2026-10-19. The owner-authorized wasm plan depends\non the shared Rust crates; this document does not independently commission\nK4 LLVM or the full K5 standalone native node.\n\nThis is the implementation plan for one Rust engine: compiled into wasm\ninstances as the in-guest engine, or run natively as a peer on dao.stream.\nIt retains the register kernel, shared Value/ownership model, scheduler and\nmodule/effect semantics. Its LLVM lowering is the sibling execution strategy\nto the compiler plan in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md). It also owns the Rust\nhost-side implementation of [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)'s\nimport surface (§6.4); those adapters do not host guest engine semantics.\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) and [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md): axioms and boundary doctrine.\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md) and [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md): abstraction and wasm implementation contract.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): shared engine semantics and K0/W0 method (§E.5).\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, state, fixtures (§10.4) and wasm gates.\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md) §7.3: offset tables, attachment and closure transport.\n- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) and [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): content/projection and A identity.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md): earlier targets, subject to the explicit amendments here.\n\n**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19:** the Level-1 remote-step/offload role and its\nboundary justification are retired. Pure execution, effect/restore conformance\nand standalone completion remain implementation slices, not separate engine\narchitectures. The doctrine is in datom.world.abi.md; the import implementation\nis in dao.stream.wasm.md. C1/C2's surviving image-lowering and local-cache laws\nare retained in §§2.7, 7 and 8 without the obsolete ratification framing.\nThe former fixed line-coordinate restriction is lifted: companion specs cite\nsections. Original CLJC source citations retain their historical verification\ndates, not newly asserted line coordinates.\n\nRevision 2's technical fixes survive: safe reference counts (§§4.1, 7.4),\nplain-data shared Images (§§4.2, 4.6), tail primitive driver returns (§7.3),\nper-machine fault containment (§§5.3, 5.5), u32 host limits (§4.2), and \"'`:live`\nin return-frame wire data (§4.4). One shared K0/W0 kit governs every dispatcher.\n\n---'\"''')\nr=r.replace('far as each level needs it, and the LLVM lowering.','far as each implementation slice needs it, the wasm host adapters, and the LLVM lowering.')\nr=block(r,'### 2.7 Design contradictions found','C3. *Everything is a continuation;', '''### 2.7 Reconciliations and surviving control laws\n\nOwner supersession, 2026-10-19: the former C1/C2 justifications and pending\nratification questions are replaced by the settled boundary doctrine in\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) and the implementation contract in\n[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md). Their surviving conclusions are:\n\n- **C1/A3:** translating an admitted held register image is a kernel execution strategy, not a replacement Direction A emitter. T4 remains separately gated.\n- **C2:** the LLVM worker uses node-local streams; artifacts are disposable local caches, never cross-node executable authority. Receivers derive their own artifacts (§§7.5, 8.3–8.4).\n\nThe independent C3/C4 control and atomicity resolutions remain:''')\nr=r.replace('The three levels of targets section\\\\n3.3 are kept, with the registers now sparse.','The following slices retain sparse registers and one engine implementation;\\\\nthe former Level-1 architecture is superseded by the 2026-10-19 ruling.')\nr=r.replace('### 6.1 Level 0: pure','### 6.1 Pure execution slice')\nr=block(r,'### 6.2 Level 1: offload','### 6.3 Level 2: standalone','''### 6.2 Effect payload and restore conformance\n\nThe kernel builds the pure R2 \"'`effect-descriptor` and `continuation-payload`\nshapes (`debruijn_register_effects.cljc:28-65`, `:83-119`) and passes effects\nto the same engine inside the wasm instance or native process. It is not a\nremote step function for a separate production Clojure engine. The CLJS/R4\nimplementation remains the conformance oracle.\n\nK2 preserves bidirectional Rust↔R4 payload/restore equality and identical\nqualified refusals, including every return frame'\"'s \"'`:live`. This is conformance\nand admissible data interchange, not an offload service. The in-instance\nscheduler owns wait sets and park/resume; only stream operations reach adapters.'\"''')\nr=r.replace('### 6.3 Level 2: standalone','### 6.3 Shared engine and standalone completion').replace('The Rust node owns the engine. Ported as a separate crate, in this','The native node and wasm instance own the same engine. Ported as a separate crate, in this')\nr=r.replace('## 7. LLVM bridging strategy','''### 6.4 Rust host implementation of the wasm stream surface\n\nImplement [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §§1–3 in the Rust\nWasmtime/Wasmer composition adapters: checked memory regions and task-local\noperational handles, canonical Jing encode/decode, exact stream outcome maps,\nand pre-operation response reservation. The signatures and buffer contract\nare specified only there, not duplicated here. Reuse \"'`yin-wire` and the shared\nrepository codecs; runtime wrappers contain no parallel kernel or engine port.\n\nThe host adapters implement stream operations over the composition'\"'s supplied\nimplementations. They do not implement guest resolve-var, primitives, stores,\nGC, continuation restoration or scheduler work: the wasm engine owns those.\nCLJS hosts implement the same language-neutral surface. Co-resident streams\nmay use an in-instance ring-buffer implementation with no host crossing.\nW0–W5 acceptance is owned by engine §E.5 and register §10.5; this host-side\nwork does not create a new K phase or change the LLVM commissioning gate.\n\n## 7. LLVM bridging strategy''',1)\nr=r.replace('At Level 2 every effect','In the shared engine every effect').replace('Level 2: scheduler','scheduler')\nr=r.replace('Rust kernel, Level 0 (targets','Rust kernel, pure (targets').replace('effects tier as offload,','effects/restore conformance,').replace('Level 1 (targets T5)','shared engine (supersedes T5)').replace('standalone node, Level 2','standalone native node')\nr=block(r,'K0. The kit targets T0 specified','K1 completion:', '''K0 is the **shared K0/W0 deliverable**, exported once from R4/CLJS, not a\nsecond interpreter or kit. Its method, differential lanes and deterministic\ntrace gates have one home in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5;\nits complete register fixture schema lives in\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.4. Export that\nkit first; preserve all fixture groups and existing goldens. The test-only\nreference interpreter proposed by targets T-D14 is superseded by the R4 oracle.\nThe wasm schedule depends on the needed shared engine subset by W3–W4, even\nthough full standalone-native completion remains K5.''')\nr=r.replace('OWNER QUESTIONS:\\\\n\\\\n1. Ratify C1/A3: may a register kernel translate the register image it\\\\n   holds, or must all native code come from resolved tuples (T4), which\\\\n   a standalone Rust node does not have?\\\\n2. Ratify C2: the LLVM worker\\\\'s streams are node-local and artifacts\\\\n   never cross nodes. Is a shared compile farm wanted later, with a\\\\n   \"'`:trusted` policy?'\"', 'OWNER QUESTIONS / RESOLUTIONS:\\\\n\\\\n1. C1/A3 is aligned with owner-authorized lowering of held admitted images (§2.7); T4 is not commissioned.\\\\n2. Node-local worker/cache scope is settled (§2.7); a shared compile farm remains deferred.')\na=r.index('client and transports; WASM as an artifact target');b=r.index('\\\\n\\\\n---',a)\nr=r[:a]+'''client and transports beyond the wasm adapter work (§6.4). The deferred wasm\nartifact target is superseded by [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md), with engine\nmethod in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5 and register fixtures/\ngates in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.'''+r[b:]\nr=r.replace('linking, sealing and collection. Level 2 is the expensive phase and\\\\n   is last for that reason; Level 1 gives effectful programs without it.','linking, sealing and collection. The wasm lane requires this shared engine\\\\n   subset before its effects/module gates; an offload substitute is not permitted.')\nr=r[:r.index('Related specs:')]+'''Related specs: [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) (doctrine),\n[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) (language-neutral adapter contract),\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5 (method),\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10 (instance/fixtures/gates),\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) (sibling wasm compiler).\n\n2026-10-19 owner decomposition: renamed and rescoped as the Rust engine plan;\nshared technical core retained, offload framing superseded, K0 fixture text\nreplaced by references to its single homes, and old fixed line citations\nreplaced by section citations in companion specs.\n'''\n# ensure preserved core\nfor a,b in [('## 4. Memory layout','## 6. Effects'),('## 7. LLVM','## 8. The Rust'),('## 9. Crate layout','## 10. Phases')]:\n before=part(orig_r,a,b); after=part(r,a,b)\n assert before.replace('Level 2: scheduler','scheduler')==after,(a,'core changed')\nout.pop('yin.vm.rust-kernel');out['yin.vm.rust-engine']=r\nout['datom.world.abi']=abi;out['dao.stream.wasm']=wasm\n# minimal implementation pointer in landed stream spec\nout['dao.stream']=out['dao.stream'].replace('specifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)','specifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\\\\n[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) specifies the wasm import-surface implementation.')\n# preserve for citation conversion next pass, save only authorized files\nfor n,s in out.items():(D/(n+'.md')).write_text(s)\n(D/'yin.vm.rust-kernel.md').unlink()\nprint('created doctrine/wasm implementation; renamed Rust plan; core preservation assertions passed')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\nD=Path('docs/design'); names=['datom.world','yin.vm.engine','yin.vm.debruijn.register','yang.wasm','yin.vm.rust-kernel','dao.stream']; old={n:(D/(n+'.md')).read_text() for n in names}; out=dict(old)\ndef part(s,a,b):return s[s.index(a):s.index(b)]\ndef block(s,a,b,t):return s[:s.index(a)]+t+'\\\\n\\\\n'+s[s.index(b):]\nmaster=old['datom.world']; doctrine=part(master,'A host boundary is a stream boundary','## Architectural Commitments')\ndoctrine=doctrine.replace('[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3','[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)')\n# preserve moved text, partition into repository-style sections\nfor a,b in [('**The system ABI.**','## 1. The system ABI\\\\n\\\\n'),('**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):**','## 2. Implementations and the taxonomy ruling\\\\n\\\\n**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):**'),('What crosses a trust domain','## 3. Custody at the boundary\\\\n\\\\nWhat crosses a trust domain'),('**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19:','## 4. Boundary ruling and supersession\\\\n\\\\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19:'),('**Effects out.**','## 6. Effects out and events in\\\\n\\\\n**Effects out.**'),('**Adapters.**','## 7. Adapter discipline\\\\n\\\\n**Adapters.**')]:doctrine=doctrine.replace(a,b)\npos=doctrine.index('## 6. Effects out')\ndoctrine=doctrine[:pos]+'''## 5. Naming and the floor\n\n“The system ABI” names only \"'`dao.stream`. Earlier qualified guest-host service\nnames are historical provenance for the dissolved protocol, not names for a\nsecond contract. Implementation specs name their implementation (WebSocket,\nwasm import surface), never “the wasm ABI”. Internal compiler linkage does not\ncreate an ecosystem boundary.\n\nThe floor is the need to obtain world facts that shipped code cannot compute.\nThat seam is immovable; the engineering frontier is movable: machine-internal\noperations can move inside the instance without changing the abstraction.\nAn in-instance stream between co-resident producers and observers needs no host\ncrossing. Changing physical placement does not remove external facts or custody.\n\nImplementations include ring-buffer, [WebSocket](./dao.stream.ws.md),\n[wasm imports](./dao.stream.wasm.md), and UDP. These implement one abstraction;\nnone adds an ABI. The import-surface representation and buffer rules belong\nonly to its implementation spec.\n\n'\"'''+doctrine[pos:]\nabi='''# datom.world ABI: the one abstraction boundary\n\nStatus: design/doctrine; owner rulings, 2026-10-19.\n\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md) owns **what** the contract is: operations,\nexhaustive outcome maps, canonical values, batch and observer semantics. This\ndocument owns **why** that contract is the only boundary: role, rulings,\nimplementation taxonomy, floor and custody. It does not redefine the operations.\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): distilled from this session's owner rulings and moved\nfrom datom.world.md, Host Boundaries. The five-round wasm consensus lineage\nis recorded in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md); subsequent owner rulings below\nsupersede its former service-protocol framing. This is the single doctrine home.\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): master axioms and commitments.\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the abstraction contract.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): execution, custody and conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): concrete machine state and fixtures.\n\n'''+doctrine\nout['datom.world']=block(master,'### Host Boundaries','## Architectural Commitments','''### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.''')\ne=old['yin.vm.engine']; adapter=part(e,'### E.3 The wasm implementation','### E.4 ')\n# single-home doctrine: implementation only points to ruling, retained technical paragraphs untouched\nadapter=adapter[adapter.index('#### E.3.1 '):]\nadapter=adapter.replace('#### E.3.1 Imported adapter surface','## 1. Imported adapter surface').replace('#### E.3.2 Outcome and buffer discipline','## 2. Outcome and buffer discipline').replace('#### E.3.3 Canonical bytes and authority','## 3. Canonical bytes and authority').replace('Jing’s canonical CBOR (§E.2.2)', 'Jing’s canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2)').replace(\\\"Jing's canonical CBOR (§E.2.2)\\\",\\\"Jing's canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2)\\\")\nwasm='''# DaoStream Wasm: the wasm implementation of the dao.stream abstraction\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented. Subordinate to\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): that document is the contract; this one\nspecifies the wasm import-surface implementation. Where they disagree, the\ncontract wins. It is a sibling implementation spec to\n[dao.stream.ws.md](./dao.stream.ws.md).\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): moved without changing the adapter contract from\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 by owner-approved decomposition.\nDoctrine and the dated supersessions have one home,\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md). The prior service-protocol tables\nremain dissolved; this move does not revive them.\n\nThe import surface exists because wasm reaches outside through imported\nfunctions over linear memory. These adapters implement dao.stream operations.\nThe specification is language-neutral: CLJS hosts and native embedders implement\nthe same surface. The Rust host-side implementation plan lives in\n[yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md) §6.4; the guest engine is not\nimplemented by those host adapters.\n\nWith co-resident producers, an in-instance ring-buffer implementation may\nservice stream operations with zero host crossings. The signatures and Jing\nbytes below specify the import-surface implementation, not a requirement that\nevery implementation serialize or cross into the host. Canonical encoding is\nowned by [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md); engine codec conformance is specified in\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2 and §E.5.\n\n'''+adapter\nout['yin.vm.engine']=block(e,'### E.3 The wasm implementation','### E.4 ','''### E.3 Wasm stream implementation reference\n\nThe adapter surface, import signatures, outcome encoding, canonical Jing bytes\nand buffer discipline live in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §§1–3.\nOwner decomposition, 2026-10-19: the former §E.3 content moved there unchanged;\n§E.4 and §E.5 retain their numbers. Boundary doctrine and supersessions live in\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md).''')\n# doctrine duplicates in existing notes become pointers; architectural supersessions stay\ns=out['yin.vm.engine'];s=s.replace('Historical rename \"'`yin.register.host.v1` → `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` is provenance,'\"\\\\nnot a live interface. The performance exception, private third codec/comparator,\\\\ntransport statuses and deferred W6 engine migration are superseded.', 'The historical rename and boundary/taxonomy supersessions are recorded only in\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §4. Deferred W6 engine migration\\\\nremains superseded by the in-instance architecture.')\nout['yin.vm.engine']=s\n# Rust rescope, core sections preserved\nr=old['yin.vm.rust-kernel']; orig_r=r\nr=block(r,'# yin.vm.rust-kernel:','## 1. Objective, scope, non-goals','''# yin.vm.rust-engine: Rust engine implementation plan\n\nStatus: design only; not implemented. Revision 2 technical core (2026-10-03),\nrescoped by owner ruling on 2026-10-19. The owner-authorized wasm plan depends\non the shared Rust crates; this document does not independently commission\nK4 LLVM or the full K5 standalone native node.\n\nThis is the implementation plan for one Rust engine: compiled into wasm\ninstances as the in-guest engine, or run natively as a peer on dao.stream.\nIt retains the register kernel, shared Value/ownership model, scheduler and\nmodule/effect semantics. Its LLVM lowering is the sibling execution strategy\nto the compiler plan in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md). It also owns the Rust\nhost-side implementation of [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md)'s\nimport surface (§6.4); those adapters do not host guest engine semantics.\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) and [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md): axioms and boundary doctrine.\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md) and [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md): abstraction and wasm implementation contract.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): shared engine semantics and K0/W0 method (§E.5).\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, state, fixtures (§10.4) and wasm gates.\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md) §7.3: offset tables, attachment and closure transport.\n- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) and [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): content/projection and A identity.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md): earlier targets, subject to the explicit amendments here.\n\n**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19:** the Level-1 remote-step/offload role and its\nboundary justification are retired. Pure execution, effect/restore conformance\nand standalone completion remain implementation slices, not separate engine\narchitectures. The doctrine is in datom.world.abi.md; the import implementation\nis in dao.stream.wasm.md. C1/C2's surviving image-lowering and local-cache laws\nare retained in §§2.7, 7 and 8 without the obsolete ratification framing.\nThe former fixed line-coordinate restriction is lifted: companion specs cite\nsections. Original CLJC source citations retain their historical verification\ndates, not newly asserted line coordinates.\n\nRevision 2's technical fixes survive: safe reference counts (§§4.1, 7.4),\nplain-data shared Images (§§4.2, 4.6), tail primitive driver returns (§7.3),\nper-machine fault containment (§§5.3, 5.5), u32 host limits (§4.2), and \"'`:live`\nin return-frame wire data (§4.4). One shared K0/W0 kit governs every dispatcher.\n\n---'\"''')\nr=r.replace('far as each level needs it, and the LLVM lowering.','far as each implementation slice needs it, the wasm host adapters, and the LLVM lowering.')\nr=block(r,'### 2.7 Design contradictions found','C3. *Everything is a continuation;', '''### 2.7 Reconciliations and surviving control laws\n\nOwner supersession, 2026-10-19: the former C1/C2 justifications and pending\nratification questions are replaced by the settled boundary doctrine in\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) and the implementation contract in\n[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md). Their surviving conclusions are:\n\n- **C1/A3:** translating an admitted held register image is a kernel execution strategy, not a replacement Direction A emitter. T4 remains separately gated.\n- **C2:** the LLVM worker uses node-local streams; artifacts are disposable local caches, never cross-node executable authority. Receivers derive their own artifacts (§§7.5, 8.3–8.4).\n\nThe independent C3/C4 control and atomicity resolutions remain:''')\nr=r.replace('The three levels of targets section\\\\n3.3 are kept, with the registers now sparse.','The following slices retain sparse registers and one engine implementation;\\\\nthe former Level-1 architecture is superseded by the 2026-10-19 ruling.')\nr=r.replace('### 6.1 Level 0: pure','### 6.1 Pure execution slice')\nr=block(r,'### 6.2 Level 1: offload','### 6.3 Level 2: standalone','''### 6.2 Effect payload and restore conformance\n\nThe kernel builds the pure R2 \"'`effect-descriptor` and `continuation-payload`\nshapes (`debruijn_register_effects.cljc:28-65`, `:83-119`) and passes effects\nto the same engine inside the wasm instance or native process. It is not a\nremote step function for a separate production Clojure engine. The CLJS/R4\nimplementation remains the conformance oracle.\n\nK2 preserves bidirectional Rust↔R4 payload/restore equality and identical\nqualified refusals, including every return frame'\"'s \"'`:live`. This is conformance\nand admissible data interchange, not an offload service. The in-instance\nscheduler owns wait sets and park/resume; only stream operations reach adapters.'\"''')\nr=r.replace('### 6.3 Level 2: standalone','### 6.3 Shared engine and standalone completion').replace('The Rust node owns the engine. Ported as a separate crate, in this','The native node and wasm instance own the same engine. Ported as a separate crate, in this')\nr=r.replace('## 7. LLVM bridging strategy','''### 6.4 Rust host implementation of the wasm stream surface\n\nImplement [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §§1–3 in the Rust\nWasmtime/Wasmer composition adapters: checked memory regions and task-local\noperational handles, canonical Jing encode/decode, exact stream outcome maps,\nand pre-operation response reservation. The signatures and buffer contract\nare specified only there, not duplicated here. Reuse \"'`yin-wire` and the shared\nrepository codecs; runtime wrappers contain no parallel kernel or engine port.\n\nThe host adapters implement stream operations over the composition'\"'s supplied\nimplementations. They do not implement guest resolve-var, primitives, stores,\nGC, continuation restoration or scheduler work: the wasm engine owns those.\nCLJS hosts implement the same language-neutral surface. Co-resident streams\nmay use an in-instance ring-buffer implementation with no host crossing.\nW0–W5 acceptance is owned by engine §E.5 and register §10.5; this host-side\nwork does not create a new K phase or change the LLVM commissioning gate.\n\n## 7. LLVM bridging strategy''',1)\nr=r.replace('At Level 2 every effect','In the shared engine every effect').replace('Level 2: scheduler','scheduler')\nr=r.replace('Rust kernel, Level 0 (targets','Rust kernel, pure (targets').replace('effects tier as offload,','effects/restore conformance,').replace('Level 1 (targets T5)','shared engine (supersedes T5)').replace('standalone node, Level 2','standalone native node')\nr=block(r,'K0. The kit targets T0 specified','K1 completion:', '''K0 is the **shared K0/W0 deliverable**, exported once from R4/CLJS, not a\nsecond interpreter or kit. Its method, differential lanes and deterministic\ntrace gates have one home in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5;\nits complete register fixture schema lives in\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.4. Export that\nkit first; preserve all fixture groups and existing goldens. The test-only\nreference interpreter proposed by targets T-D14 is superseded by the R4 oracle.\nThe wasm schedule depends on the needed shared engine subset by W3–W4, even\nthough full standalone-native completion remains K5.''')\nr=r.replace('OWNER QUESTIONS:\\\\n\\\\n1. Ratify C1/A3: may a register kernel translate the register image it\\\\n   holds, or must all native code come from resolved tuples (T4), which\\\\n   a standalone Rust node does not have?\\\\n2. Ratify C2: the LLVM worker\\\\'s streams are node-local and artifacts\\\\n   never cross nodes. Is a shared compile farm wanted later, with a\\\\n   \"'`:trusted` policy?'\"', 'OWNER QUESTIONS / RESOLUTIONS:\\\\n\\\\n1. C1/A3 is aligned with owner-authorized lowering of held admitted images (§2.7); T4 is not commissioned.\\\\n2. Node-local worker/cache scope is settled (§2.7); a shared compile farm remains deferred.')\na=r.index('client and transports; WASM as an artifact target');b=r.index('\\\\n\\\\n---',a)\nr=r[:a]+'''client and transports beyond the wasm adapter work (§6.4). The deferred wasm\nartifact target is superseded by [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md), with engine\nmethod in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5 and register fixtures/\ngates in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.'''+r[b:]\nr=r.replace('linking, sealing and collection. Level 2 is the expensive phase and\\\\n   is last for that reason; Level 1 gives effectful programs without it.','linking, sealing and collection. The wasm lane requires this shared engine\\\\n   subset before its effects/module gates; an offload substitute is not permitted.')\nr=r[:r.index('Related specs:')]+'''Related specs: [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) (doctrine),\n[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) (language-neutral adapter contract),\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5 (method),\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10 (instance/fixtures/gates),\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) (sibling wasm compiler).\n\n2026-10-19 owner decomposition: renamed and rescoped as the Rust engine plan;\nshared technical core retained, offload framing superseded, K0 fixture text\nreplaced by references to its single homes, and old fixed line citations\nreplaced by section citations in companion specs.\n'''\n# ensure preserved core\nfor a,b in [('## 4. Memory layout','## 6. Effects'),('## 7. LLVM','## 8. The Rust'),('## 9. Crate layout','## 10. Phases')]:\n before=part(orig_r,a,b); after=part(r,a,b)\n assert before.replace('Level 2: scheduler','scheduler')==after,(a,'core changed')\nout.pop('yin.vm.rust-kernel');out['yin.vm.rust-engine']=r\nout['datom.world.abi']=abi;out['dao.stream.wasm']=wasm\n# minimal implementation pointer in landed stream spec\nout['dao.stream']=out['dao.stream'].replace('specifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)','specifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\\\\n[dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) specifies the wasm import-surface implementation.')\n# preserve for citation conversion next pass, save only authorized files\nfor n,s in out.items():(D/(n+'.md')).write_text(s)\n(D/'yin.vm.rust-kernel.md').unlink()\nprint('created doctrine/wasm implementation; renamed Rust plan; core preservation assertions passed')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"created doctrine/wasm implementation; renamed Rust plan; core preservation assertions passed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '565,605p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '120,150p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '1230,1258p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; rg -n 'rust-kernel|lines [0-9]|at [0-9]|§E\\\\.3|Level [012]|offload|retired|1–1334|original lines' docs/design/{yin.vm.engine,yin.vm.debruijn.register,yang.wasm,yin.vm.rust-engine,dao.stream.wasm,datom.world.abi}.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '565,605p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '120,150p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '1230,1258p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; rg -n 'rust-kernel|lines [0-9]|at [0-9]|§E\\\\.3|Level [012]|offload|retired|1–1334|original lines' docs/design/{yin.vm.engine,yin.vm.debruijn.register,yang.wasm,yin.vm.rust-engine,dao.stream.wasm,datom.world.abi}.md\"","aggregated_output":"The in-instance engine owns run-loop/scheduler-round, step budgets, ready/wait\nqueues and terminal checks. A step performs exactly one guest instruction; run\nkeeps the original ready-queue/terminal ordering and returns on the same blocked,\nparked or halted states. Blocking is a normal result. Inspection/test snapshots\nare immutable data, not an external engine service contract. Runtime start/drive\nexports may only enter the in-instance driver; they are lifecycle controls, not\napplication callbacks. No wasm stack survives a wait.\n\n#### E.4.2 Single-engine-port rule and Rust reconciliation\n\nThe production engine **is** the Rust design's `yin-value`, `yin-scalar`,\n`yin-image`, `yin-kernel`, `yin-prims`, `yin-engine`, `yin-wire` workspace\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1134–1158`), compiled to\n`wasm32-unknown-unknown` and linked with compiled register bodies. The internal\nhandle facade adapts values, never reimplements semantics. Native wrappers\nimplement only stream adapters and runtime mechanics. No parallel engine port\nis permitted to bypass a crate's missing wasm support.\n\n“One transition implementation, two dispatchers” applies to the Rust interpreter,\ncompiled wasm bodies and future LLVM helpers (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:991–995, 1308–1311`).\nGenerated control flow uses shared transition helpers for call/return/restore/\neffects; no wrapper duplicates them. CLJS necessarily has a language-port\nimplementation: it remains the shared-kit oracle, not literally the same binary.\nCheck-then-commit, refused-step equality and per-machine fault containment remain\nmandatory (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:639–713`). Panics are kernel defects,\nnot success outcomes. W0/W1 must prove trap containment with this wasm profile;\nnever rely on wasm exceptions or unwinding. Safe checked slices/Value APIs and\nthe Rust design's reviewed boundary crates retain the unsafe-code boundary\n(lines 1154–1158).\n\nRust revision 2's design-only status is unchanged. This wasm plan supersedes its\nDEFERRED wasm artifact item (lines 1294–1300), not the K3 review gate for K4 or\nunrelated standalone-node/transports work. The required K-phase library subset\nis now a **wasm build dependency**, including Level-2 engine work by W3–W4.\nIt does not authorize a separate full K5 node. A remains sole code identity;\nR is executable checksum despite older Rust topology wording (lines 282–318,\n997–1011). u32 Rust indices retain checked host limits (434–448, 1245–1246)\nagainst 64-bit stored pc coordinates, never truncation. Rust public step still\ninterprets (677–678); generated one-step mode is a separate dispatcher.\nShared-reference traversal stabilization below remains an explicitly scoped\nwasm prerequisite outside the Rust phases' CLJC-edit exclusion (93–95, 1164–1165).\n\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2 is an authority for\nthe native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\ndecisions at 1234–1265. It remains **design-only, not authorized for implementation**. This\nspecification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) record the owner-authorized wasm plan;\nthey neither commission K4 LLVM nor the unrelated standalone Rust node/transports. Where native\nownership, Value, transition reuse, fault isolation and crate boundaries govern, this plan is\nsubordinate to that design. Crate/type names below are proposed by that document, not claims of\nimplemented Rust symbols.\n\nThis plan explicitly **supersedes only the DEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`). Its other deferred items and K3-owner-review\nprerequisite for K4 remain. The following narrower reconciliations prevent silent\ncontradictions:\n\n- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines 282–318; §7.5, lines 997–1011). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\n- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (lines 93–95, 1164–1165). The deterministic engine traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (lines 677–678). Keep that rule for the Rust interpreter lane. The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a different dispatcher used to verify its generated execution; it does not silently replace Rust's public `step`. Optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law.\n- The in-instance wasm engine needs Level-2 engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§6.3, lines 748–781; §10, lines 1170–1182). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); a Level-1 offload service cannot satisfy in-instance wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\n\nOwner question 1 (lines 1282–1284) and C1/A3 (lines 243–258, 1275–1277) align with the owner's\ninstruction to lower held register images: wasm is a kernel execution strategy over an admitted\nimage, not a replacement source emitter. Do not reopen that choice or infer authorization for\nLLVM. The node-local cache choice aligns with question 2; compile farms remain out of scope.\nWorker envelope choice and K3 commissioning threshold remain with the LLVM work; W0's declared\nscalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\nat 1285–1292).\n\n---\n> Private TLV/comparator/status/refetch machinery and W6 engine migration are\n> deleted. Fixtures and register transition semantics survive unchanged.\n> Owner taxonomy clarification, 2026-10-19: dao.stream is an abstraction\n> boundary; ring-buffer, websocket, UDP and wasm import surfaces implement it.\n> Stream operations with co-resident producers may use an in-instance\n> ring-buffer with zero host crossings; the import adapter contract is unchanged.\n> Wasmer's full-suite gate moves from W4 to W5 under the revised schedule.\n>\n> Earlier decomposition/fold provenance remains: engine method/implementation live in\n> engine §E; this section holds register state, fixtures and gates. The dated\n> correction of historical `:store-updates` remains: ready entries merge\n> `:resource-updates` into private `:resources`, refusing non-keyword keys with\n> `{:rule :resource-update-key}`.\n\nAuthorities and companion specifications for §10:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution, and A→R derivation.\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables, and module transport.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): scheduler, effects, custody, and (`§E.1–§E.5`) the in-instance engine and wasm implementation of dao.stream, and K0/W0 differential conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, `Value` ownership, transition reuse, and LLVM sibling relationship.\n- [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md): lowering register images to WebAssembly modules, the 10-region linear-memory layout, and all 23 opcode lowerings linked to the in-instance engine.\n\n### 10.1 Register machine state and the register instance of the synchronization barrier\n\nThis is the register instance (contract version 4 / \"r2\", 23 opcodes) of the\nin-instance engine in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.1–§E.5.\n[yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md) compiles admitted register images and links the\nsame Rust engine into wasm under Node/Wasmtime/Wasmer. The CLJS kernel is the\noracle. Arena/Value facade and both repository codecs live inside the instance;\ndocs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:10:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 by owner-approved decomposition.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:25:**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19:** the Level-1 remote-step/offload role and its\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:26:boundary justification are retired. Pure execution, effect/restore conformance\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:725:and admissible data interchange, not an offload service. The in-instance\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:1316:   subset before its effects/module gates; an offload substitute is not permitted.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:1332:shared technical core retained, offload framing superseded, K0 fixture text\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:18:implementation specified in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. Its former\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:23:implementation specified in engine §E.3; a co-resident in-instance ring-buffer\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:30:Retired standalone wasm/kit documents remain retired. Unsuperseded r5 semantic\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:42:- [yin.vm.rust-kernel.md](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:121:[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`](./yin.vm.rust-kernel.md) revision 2 is an authority for\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:122:the native implementation: status and subordination at lines 3–35, crates at 1134–1158,\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:123:decisions at 1234–1265. It remains **design-only, not authorized for implementation**. This\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:132:(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1294–1300`). Its other deferred items and K3-owner-review\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:136:- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§3, lines 282–318; §7.5, lines 997–1011). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:137:- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (lines 434–448, 1245–1246); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:138:- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC