docs/design/dao.star.md, docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md (not mine)\n11212:Tree: master@0e549b72 at writing (other seats move it); worktree datomworld-ad2 (branch admission-ad2, UNCOMMITTED AD-2 work by an opus run); main tree carries other seats' uncommitted log entries and docs/design/dao.star.md, docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md (not mine)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:1:# yin.vm.rust-engine: Rust engine implementation plan\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:25:**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19:** the Level-1 remote-step/offload role and its\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:725:and admissible data interchange, not an offload service. The in-instance\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:1281:DEFERRED: a kernel-owned `repr(C)` counted object header that would\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:1316:   subset before its effects/module gates; an offload substitute is not permitted.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md:1332:shared technical core retained, offload framing superseded, K0 fixture text\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:363:now operate inside wasm; §E.3 points to the extracted implementation; §E.4 hosts only\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:370:[yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md), [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:433:explicitly not NaN-boxed (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §4.1 and §11 K-D2).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:439:materializing constants per task (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §4.2 and §4.6).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:507:`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §9, extended for the current\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:549:Owner decomposition, 2026-10-19: the former §E.3 content moved there unchanged;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:577:(`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §9), compiled to\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:584:compiled wasm bodies and future LLVM helpers (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §7.4 and §12 risk 2).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:589:mandatory (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §§5.3–5.5). Panics are kernel defects,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:596:DEFERRED wasm artifact item (Rust engine §11 Deferred), not the K3 review gate for K4 or\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:700:artifact-cache policy (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §7.5, §8.4 and §11 K-D7; [`yang.wasm.md`](./yang.wasm.md) §5;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:719:[`yin.vm.rust-engine.md`](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md), this specification\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:723:second kit (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §10 K0, equivalence law at §7.2). The execution specifications retain their respective\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:795:Both repository codecs (§E.2.2) must match frozen bytes/hashes across CLJS and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:863:(`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §10). Full register slice\ndocs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:887:requirement to load Clojure inside wasm. Rust-design references use sections of [yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md).\ndocs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:10:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 by owner-approved decomposition.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:19:[yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md) §6.4; the guest engine is not\ndocs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:27:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2 and §E.5.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:98:In the import-surface implementation, requests and outcome data use exactly Jing's canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2), with its\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:40:- [yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:119:[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md`](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md) retains the revision 2 technical core and is an authority for\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:130:This plan **supersedes the DEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:131:(`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §11 Deferred). The Rust engine rescope also adds the host adapters (§6.4); unrelated deferred items and K3-owner-review\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:139:- The in-instance wasm engine needs shared engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§§6.3, 10). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); the superseded offload role cannot satisfy in-instance wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:276:`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §7.2 (§7.2): from equal reachable boundary states they\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:479:Artifact policy is also shared with LLVM: `yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §7.5, §8.4 and §11 K-D7. Wasm binaries are disposable **node-local caches**, never dao.jing\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:552:references, checked against the current tree for this distillation. Rust-design references now use sections of yin.vm.rust-engine.md under the\n# yang.wasm: compiler implementation plan — register bytecode to WebAssembly\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented.\n\nConsensus provenance (2026-10-18): gpt-6-astra, author; gemini-4-argon,\nreviewer; five review rounds, APPROVE on r5. Author thread:\n`01a14f30-ca56-7530-bb93-3a870eca7a7b`; reviewer thread:\n`f365ef1d-caa2-4379-8e96-51e64ca36af8`. This specification distills the\ncompiler half of the r5 consensus, subject to the owner supersession below: semantic\nfidelity, portability, determinism and Rust-kernel reconciliation. It\ndoes not report an implementation or runtime acceptance result.\n\n**Owner supersession, 2026-10-19 (boundary clarification):** this is the\ncompiler implementation plan for register bytecode → wasm. The earlier\nbody-lane-first architecture and `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` service target\n(historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`) are superseded. Compile the\nshared Rust engine crates into the instance; imports are only the dao.stream\nimplementation specified in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §§1–3.\nThe historical service-protocol and taxonomy rulings have one home in\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §§2–5. Engine-in-guest is mandatory,\nnot a W6 experiment. Owner decomposition, 2026-10-19: this compiler plan now\nreferences the extracted doctrine and stream implementation specs.\n\nThe 2026-10-19 decomposition remains: engine representation/conformance\nmethod live in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E; register state,\nfixtures and acceptance gates in [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\n§10. This plan owns compiler mechanics, not host service implementation.\nRetired standalone wasm/kit documents remain retired. Unsuperseded r5 semantic\nlaws, opcode rows, fixture obligations and artifact rules remain binding.\n\n## Authorities\n\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): foundational axioms and invariants.\n- [yang.antlr.md](./yang.antlr.md): frontend compilation into the Universal AST.\n- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md): Universal AST content and projection discipline.\n- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution and A→R derivation.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): register contract, live sets, sparse continuations, and (`§10`) the register-machine WebAssembly execution instance (the eight materialized register fields, sparse continuations on wasm, `register-restore`, register fixture schema, and register-specific W0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 gates).\n- [yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md): admission, offset tables and module transport.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): scheduler, effects, custody, and (`§E.1–§E.5`) the in-instance engine, reference to the wasm stream implementation, and K0/W0 conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md): shared Rust crates, Value ownership, transition reuse and LLVM relationship.\n\nSource precedence and the explicit Rust-design reconciliations below are\nretained from r5.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Pipeline position, decision and source precedence\n\nPipeline position:\n\n    yang source --(yang.antlr)--> Universal AST --(project)--> semantic vector A\n        --(yin.vm.semantic/derive register-image)--> register image R\n        --(yang.wasm, this spec)--> WebAssembly module (disposable local artifact)\n\n**A is the only code identity.** R is the register image checksum. The\n\nEach phase adds files only under `src/host/rust/` and `test/`; none\nedits an existing `.cljc` namespace.\n\n    +----+-------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    | Ph | Deliverable                   | Depends on                     |\n    +----+-------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    | K0 | host conformance kit (targets | R1, R2, R4: all landed         |\n    |    | T0), exported from R4         |                                |\n    | K1 | Rust kernel, pure (targets | K0                             |\n    |    | T1), incl. attach-image       |                                |\n    | K2 | effects/restore conformance,      | K1                             |\n    |    | shared engine (supersedes T5)          |                                |\n    | K3 | benchmark report: K1 vs R4 on | K1                             |\n    |    | JVM vs whole-node AOT of two  |                                |\n    |    | hand-translated corpus images |                                |\n    | K4 | translator, inline runtime,   | K2; K3 reviewed by the owner   |\n    |    | worker, native path           |                                |\n    | K5 | standalone native node      | K2; dao.stream Rust client     |\n    |    | (targets T6)                  | (not designed here)            |\n    +----+-------------------------------+--------------------------------+\n\nK0 is the **shared K0/W0 deliverable**, exported once from R4/CLJS, not a\nsecond interpreter or kit. Its method, differential lanes and deterministic\ntrace gates have one home in [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5;\nits complete register fixture schema lives in\n[yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.4. Export that\nkit first; preserve all fixture groups and existing goldens. The test-only\nreference interpreter proposed by targets T-D14 is superseded by the R4 oracle.\nThe wasm schedule depends on the needed shared engine subset by W3–W4, even\nthough full standalone-native completion remains K5.\n\nK1 completion: every pure fixture passes; R equal; refusals equal by\nrule; no recursion in `step1` or `run`; constant native stack and one\nfile per live activation on the tail fixtures; atomicity fixtures pass;\nno panic; a fault-injection test (a deliberately corrupted decoded\nimage, bypassing the validator) yields `:kernel-invariant` refusals and\na poisoned machine at worst, never a process exit, while a second\nmachine on the same node runs to completion.\n\nK2 completion: for every effect fixture, the Rust kernel's payload and\nmodule installation need physical-runtime probes. Predicate/codec drift is caught\nby the oracle kit. Measure in-instance code size, dispatch and collection costs;\nnone justifies restoring a second semantic service protocol.\n\n#### E.5.5 Reference conventions and open items\n\nCLJC helper names denote oracle semantics and shared Rust equivalents, not a\nrequirement to load Clojure inside wasm. Rust-design references use sections of [yin.vm.rust-engine.md](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md).\nAdapter names in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §1 are proposed imports.\n\nThe owner ruling resolves the former compiled-call/service split: generated\ncontrol invokes the one in-instance transition implementation. It also removes\nthe former response-refetch sequencing question with that protocol. Remaining\nW0 gates are wasm32 build/profile feasibility, bounded response reservations,\nand a frozen internal structural ordering for noncanonical values (§E.4.3).\nDo not invent a third codec to answer the last question.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
