{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '705,718p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '114,148p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '588,608p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; rg -n '§E\\\\.[12345]' docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md; rg -n 'Level|offload|C1|C2|R-as|R is|identity' docs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md | tail -25\"","aggregated_output":"materialized boundary, with no pending mutation or active guest stack.\n\n### E.5 Conformance method, guest-agnostic acceptance gates, toolchain pins, non-goals, risks, and open items\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the\nconformance **method** previously housed in `docs/design/yin.vm.conformance-kit.md`\n(`§1–§5`, `§8`) is folded into this section as the acceptance specification of\nthe in-instance engine; the register-specific fixture schema and register\nW0–W6 / Rust K1–K5 lane gates live in\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) `§10.4–§10.5`.)*\n\n#### E.5.1 Oracle export, differential execution lanes, and trace comparison method\n\nThe K0/W0 conformance kit is the single **shared deliverable** of\n- Current `vm/machine-data?`, used by register restore and payload validation, recursively accepts kernel-minted Closure/Continuation wrappers by their payloads, as well as exact-integer carriers. It is not identical to `plain-data?`. Arbitrary host functions/errors remain invalid. Preserve the predicate, not an approximate JSON test.\n- The current module-slice encoder in `engine/encoder` explicitly refuses reified and parked continuations with `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:non-canonicalizable`. Thus universal linker transport of every register continuation is not already implemented. This lane must support the existing native payload where admissible, and preserve qualified refusals elsewhere; it must not silently promise a new universal migration mechanism.\n\n### 1.1 Rust-kernel authority, scope and explicit reconciliations\n\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-engine.md`](./yin.vm.rust-engine.md) retains the revision 2 technical core and is an authority for\nthe shared implementation: opening status/authorities, crate workspace (§9)\nand decisions (§11). It remains **design-only**; the wasm plan depends on its\nshared crates without commissioning unrelated K4/full-K5 work. This\nspecification, [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) (§E.1–§E.5), and\n[`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) (§10) record the owner-authorized wasm plan;\nthey neither commission K4 LLVM nor the unrelated standalone Rust node/transports. Where native\nownership, Value, transition reuse, fault isolation and crate boundaries govern, this plan is\nsubordinate to that design. Crate/type names below are proposed by that document, not claims of\nimplemented Rust symbols.\n\nThis plan **supersedes the DEFERRED item “WASM as an artifact target”**\n(`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §11 Deferred). The Rust engine rescope also adds the host adapters (§6.4); unrelated deferred items and K3-owner-review\nprerequisite for K4 remain. The following narrower reconciliations prevent silent\ncontradictions:\n\n- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§§3, 7.5). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\n- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (§4.2 and §11 K-D2b); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\n- Rust phases exclude existing CLJC edits (§§1, 10). The deterministic engine traversal policy in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.3 (r5 §4.1.2) is a separately identified **wasm-driven shared-reference stabilization prerequisite**, outside that K-phase restriction; it remains an explicit scoped amendment for implementation review, not permission for a second native ordering policy. Export a versioned extension of the common kit after that shared change, preserving original goldens/history. This specification changes no source.\n- Rust public `step` always interprets (§5.3). Keep that rule for the Rust interpreter lane. The wasm backend's explicit one-instruction test/inspection mode is a different dispatcher used to verify its generated execution; it does not silently replace Rust's public `step`. Optimized wasm run chunks obey the same boundary equivalence law.\n- The in-instance wasm engine needs shared engine services in W2–W4 even though the Rust standalone-node roadmap leaves K5 last (§§6.3, 10). Develop/reuse those **same crates** to the slice's required coverage ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.2); the superseded offload role cannot satisfy in-instance wasm parity. This schedules the necessary library subset for wasm, not the full K5 node or a new Rust network client.\n\nOwner resolution 1 (§11) and C1/A3 (§2.7 and §11) align with the owner's\ninstruction to lower held register images: wasm is a kernel execution strategy over an admitted\nimage, not a replacement source emitter. Do not reopen that choice or infer authorization for\nLLVM. The node-local cache choice aligns with question 2; compile farms remain out of scope.\nWorker envelope choice and K3 commissioning threshold remain with the LLVM work; W0's declared\nscalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\nin §11).\n\nCheck-then-commit, refused-step equality and per-machine fault containment remain\nmandatory (`yin.vm.rust-engine.md` §§5.3–5.5). Panics are kernel defects,\nnot success outcomes. W0/W1 must prove trap containment with this wasm profile;\nnever rely on wasm exceptions or unwinding. Safe checked slices/Value APIs and\nthe Rust design's reviewed boundary crates retain the unsafe-code boundary\n(Rust engine §9).\n\nRust revision 2's design-only status is unchanged. This wasm plan supersedes its\nDEFERRED wasm artifact item (Rust engine §11 Deferred), not the K3 review gate for K4 or\nunrelated standalone-node/transports work. The required K-phase library subset\nis now a **wasm build dependency**, including shared engine work by W3–W4.\nIt does not authorize a separate full K5 node. A remains sole code identity;\nR is executable checksum despite older Rust topology wording (Rust engine §§3, 7.5). u32 Rust indices retain checked host limits (Rust engine §4.2 and §11 K-D2b)\nagainst 64-bit stored pc coordinates, never truncation. Rust public step still\ninterprets (Rust engine §5.3); generated one-step mode is a separate dispatcher.\nShared-reference traversal stabilization below remains an explicitly scoped\nwasm prerequisite outside the Rust phases' CLJC-edit exclusion (Rust engine §§1, 10).\n\n#### E.4.3 Deterministic engine traversal prerequisite\n\nSource verification found real unsorted map/set walks that can mint ids or\n10:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 by owner-approved decomposition.\n27:[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2 and §E.5.\n98:In the import-surface implementation, requests and outcome data use exactly Jing's canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2), with its\n240:Owner supersession, 2026-10-19: the former C1/C2 justifications and pending\n245:- **C1/A3:** translating an admitted held register image is a kernel execution strategy, not a replacement Direction A emitter. T4 remains separately gated.\n246:- **C2:** the LLVM worker uses node-local streams; artifacts are disposable local caches, never cross-node executable authority. Receivers derive their own artifacts (§§7.5, 8.3–8.4).\n298:   identity, recomputed at will).\n392:        rows:   Vec<Row>,               // offset table [identity offset len]\n513:    | code space    | image, hash, rows              | kernel; by identity |\n531:loaded artifacts, keyed by offset-table row identity (7.5). It is a host\n705:the former Level-1 architecture is superseded by the 2026-10-19 ruling.\n725:and admissible data interchange, not an offload service. The in-instance\n751:gates in order: format and row identity (`:image`, never `:hash`),\n793:`(row identity, translator version, ABI version)` gives equal bytes.\n984:no identity (targets 3.5), and it changes nothing in 7.2: the same\n994:### 7.5 Artifact identity and the code cache\n996:    key = (row identity, target triple, translator version, ABI version)\n998:- Row identity is the offset-table row's identity: R of that image\n1007:  the machine as the `native` resource. Lookup is by row identity at\n1049:       identity the batch or the link response claims\n1074:  values it appends. Correlation is by identity in the data.\n1168:| K2 | Effects/restore conformance in the shared engine; supersedes T5 offload framing | K1 |\n1230:   carry no code space, and restore by row identity.\n1243:7. K-D7. Artifacts are node-local caches keyed by `(row identity,\n1259:  emitter, and is permitted (contradiction C1).\n1264:1. C1/A3 is aligned with owner-authorized lowering of held admitted images (§2.7); T4 is not commissioned.\n1307:   subset before its effects/module gates; an offload substitute is not permitted.\n1323:shared technical core retained, offload framing superseded, K0 fixture text\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
